# rip-cage protected paths — the mount-side containment floor (ADR-031 D2/D5(d)).
#
# WHERE rc READS THIS FROM: `rc up` takes the first of $RC_PROTECTED_PATHS,
# ~/.config/rip-cage/protected-paths, then this shipped default beside the rc
# script itself (<rc install dir>/share/rip-cage/protected-paths) — always a
# host-side path outside every cage mount, never a path a caged agent can edit.
#
# This is DEFAULT CONFIGURATION, not code. Copy it to
# ~/.config/rip-cage/protected-paths and edit it there; a new credential store
# is a line in your copy, never an `rc` change.
#
# WHAT rc DOES WITH EACH LINE — one name per line, matched by exact path
# COMPONENT (not a glob, not a substring):
#
#   (a) a cage config that mounts a listed path DIRECTLY makes `rc up` refuse
#       to launch, before any msb call;
#   (b) a listed path found INSIDE a mounted tree is auto-covered — a file with
#       an empty read-only file mount, a directory with an empty tmpfs mount —
#       so the cage sees the name but none of the content;
#   (c) if this file cannot be read, `rc up` aborts before any msb call. There
#       is no fail-open path and no opt-out.
#
# If a cover cannot be expressed for an entry, `rc up` refuses rather than
# launching uncovered.
#
# SCAN DEPTH: the (b) scan walks each mounted tree to a bounded depth
# (RC_PROTECTED_SCAN_DEPTH, default 2) — deep enough for a credential at a
# project root or one level into a monorepo package, shallow enough that
# mounting a large tree does not stall every launch. Depth is a latency
# tradeoff, not a claim of exhaustive reach.
#
# Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are ignored. Leading/trailing
# whitespace is stripped.

# --- SSH ---------------------------------------------------------------
.ssh
id_rsa
id_ed25519
id_ecdsa
private_key

# --- Cloud providers ---------------------------------------------------
.aws
.azure
.gcloud
.kube
.docker

# --- Signing and package registries ------------------------------------
.gnupg
.gpg
.netrc
.npmrc
.pypirc

# --- Generic credential files ------------------------------------------
credentials
.secret

# --- Environment files -------------------------------------------------
# ADR-023 D4 kept .env OUT of the old denylist because the only response
# available then was a refusal, and refusing every project that carries a .env
# would have been hostile to normal work. Under ADR-031 D2 a .env inside a
# mounted tree is auto-covered instead of refused, so the cost of listing it is
# an empty file where a secret used to be — not a blocked launch.
.env
.env.local
.envrc
